.TH JOINTEST 1
.SH NAME
jointest \- regression test for join(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jointest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Jointest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/join.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR runstring(args,\ in1,\ in2) ,
and checks a representative subset of the programs used to develop
.IR join (1)
against golden output: the default matched format,
.BR -a / -v
unpairable-line handling (including that
.B -v
suppresses matched output entirely, unlike
.BR -a ),
duplicate-key cross products,
.BR -o / -e
field selection and replacement,
.BR -1 / -2
field selection, and the
.B -t
separator. Every expected string was checked once against the host's
real
.IR join .
.PP
Also checks a real bug this file's own probing caught: an early version
of
.B -o
field-order handling produced its fields backwards
.RB ( "0,1.2,2.2"
came out
.BR "2.2,1.2,0" )
from a double-reversal mistake, found by comparing
.B -o
output against the host rather than by inspection.
.PP
Prints one line per failure to standard error, a final
.IR N " passed, " N " failed"
summary to standard output, and exits with a non-nil status if
anything failed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/jointest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR join (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I join
in-process via its
.B runstring
seam, not as a spawned subprocess reading real files and standard
input/output \(em so it does not exercise
.IR join 's
own
.BR init ,
argument parsing, file I/O, or the
.B -
(stdin) file argument at all.
